
1. What are the basic rules of XML you applied in this lab?

The basic rules of XML that I applied for this lab include using proper opening and closing tags, following proper nesting rules, and having one root element that keeps all the data organized. I have also tried to use clear and consistent tag names so as to make them easier to read.

2. Why does XML need a single root element?

This is because XML requires at least a single root element since it acts as the main container that holds all other elements. Otherwise, the structure will not be well-formed and the parser will not know where the document starts and ends. The root element will make sure that the data remains organized and properly linked.

3. How does XML ensure interoperability?

With XML, interoperability is guaranteed since a unified format is utilized that other systems and platforms can easily read and understand. Since XML is text-based and self-descriptive, it facilitates the exchange of data across different applications because they are independent of any programming language or environment.
